This extension runs, as separate programs, software under other licences. Each ships with its own
licence files. It bundles no Python.

- opentide, in dist/opentide/ of each platform package (linux, darwin and win32, x64 and arm64): the npm
  package @opentide/opentide (opentidehq/opentide: the opentide SDK and command line, the Rust core as a
  Node-API addon) with the native addon for that platform, licensed under the EUPL-1.2. It ships with
  its LICENSE and runs as a process of its own (`<VS Code's executable> dist/opentide/cli.js`, as
  Node.js); the extension does not load it into its own process. The licences of the Rust crates
  compiled into it are those of opentide's release THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.html for the same version. The
  universal package carries none.

- The opentide engine (WASM), in dist/engine/ of every package: opentidehq/language-server's language
  server built for WebAssembly (opentide-wasm, from its opentide-wasm-web release zip), licensed under the
  EUPL-1.2. It ships with its LICENSE, NOTICE and THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.html (the Rust crates compiled
  into it), and the licences of the wasi-libc it links (dist/engine/licenses/). dist/engine/worker.js
  bundles its wasm-bindgen glue, from the same zip.
- The TextMate grammars in syntaxes/ other than opentide-config.tmLanguage.json are generated by
  opentidehq/language-server and copied from its release highlights zip; they are EUPL-1.2, see
  syntaxes/LICENSE.language-server and syntaxes/NOTICE.language-server.
- syntaxes/opentide-config.tmLanguage.json is copied from Even Better TOML (tamasfe/taplo) under the
  MIT License, see THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.

No package carries a Python runtime. A platform package runs the opentide it carries unless
opentide.path, opentide.interpreter or opentide.uv.usePin names another; the universal package runs the
opentide installed in the workspace (its `opentide` command, else `python -m opentide`), or the one
opentide.path names, else the engine (WASM) for editor features.
